#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""program-rebalance -- the workout program's VOLUME ALLOCATOR and EDIT GATE.

WHY THIS EXISTS
---------------
Until today the agent could only JUDGE a program (rx-calc --volume-audit emits
below_floor / in_band / above_ceiling) -- it could not GENERATE the fix. So
every rebalance was hand-authored by a human or by the pack maintainer, which is exactly the
"local slot CRUD, no global pass" pathology recorded as root #31 (design:
design notes 2026-08-19 (program holistic redesign), sub-problem
1 "volume allocation"). The owner's correction was explicit: the agent must not
hand-author the program itself -- it must author the RULES the program engine
follows, and let the engine produce the plan.

So the RULE lives here, in code, and the machine produces the plan.

WHAT IT DOES / DOES NOT DO
--------------------------
  * It PROPOSES. It never writes program.yaml. There is deliberately no
    --apply flag: the owner approves a diff, and the landing edit is a separate,
    human-gated step. (DESIGN 7.1 Q4: the program is the owner's authority.)
  * It never invents an exercise. A brand-new slot is only ever proposed from
    exercises that already exist in kit_life.db (exercise_muscle /
    exercise_subregion). If the DB holds no exercise that can serve a deficient
    region, the allocator says NO_CANDIDATE and hands the question back --
    it does not make up a movement name.
  * It never re-derives a rule that already exists. Bands, per-muscle
    accounting, sub-region accounting, focus resolution, fatigue cost and the
    slot-priority ordering all come from rx-calc. A second copy of any of them
    would be the two-ledger pathology this codebase relapses into.

BANDS ARE INPUTS, NOT TRUTH
---------------------------
rx-calc's own comment on the sub-region bands reads: "NO verified
per-subregion numbers exist -- volume-research 2026-07-18 MAJOR GAP", and the
group bands are marked PROVISIONAL. On top of that they are a HYPERTROPHY
yardstick (MEV/MAV/MRV), while the owner's recorded goal (2026-08-18) is
FUNCTIONAL -- "기능성 재조성", "근력·퍼포먼스 우선". Therefore:

  * The OBJECTIVE is explicit and swappable (--objective / --objective-weights).
    Band compliance is one term among several, not the definition of "good".
  * The default objective is `functional`: hard band FLOORS are respected
    (they are the health/minimum-stimulus anchor), the band TARGET is only a
    faint pull, main/focus/rehab slots are protected from trimming, and
    session length is a real cost.
  * `band_center` (chase the MAV target) is available but is NOT the default,
    precisely because it encodes the hypertrophy reading of the bands.
  * Every band used is echoed in the report with its provenance so the owner
    can reject the yardstick itself, and `program.yaml volume_bands` (already
    honoured by rx-calc resolve_band) overrides any of them.

MODES
-----
  program-rebalance --allocate <program.yaml> <today> <db>   (default mode)
      Greedy allocation proposal. Exit 0 = no unresolved hard violation,
      3 = proposal produced but something needs an owner decision, 1 = fatal.

  program-rebalance --gate <program.yaml> <today> <db> [--baseline <path>]
      Invariant gate for a program EDIT. Fails loudly (exit 4) when the edit
      breaks a slot-priority invariant, introduces a NEW band violation
      relative to the baseline, or breaks the COORDINATION (unilateral) rule --
      see the UNILATERAL FLOOR section. Exit 0 = clean, 1 = fatal.

THE COORDINATION FLOOR IS NOT ADVISORY
--------------------------------------
The owner's rule, in their own words (2026-08-24):

    "프리웨이트에서도 협응을 많이 요구하는 것들이 있잖아 주로 편측 운동이 그렇고.
     그냥 SBD만 하면 몸이 망가지더라고"

Two structural edits are REFUSED outright (exit 4): moving a unilateral slot
behind the cut line, and swapping a unilateral movement for a bilateral one.
Falling below the share floor WARNS. The floor itself is a judgement value
derived from the owner's own program and history, and it is an INPUT
(--unilateral-floor-share / --unilateral-min-per-day), never a hardcoded truth.

THE COORDINATION AXIS HAS TWO SUB-MEASURES, AND LOAD IS NOT ONE OF THEM
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
The block above measures ONE of them. That was the whole axis until now, and
reporting one sub-measure under the axis's own name is how the other one stayed
invisible.

The correction is the owner's, and it arrived as a counterexample (2026-08-24):

    "필라테스는 이 연결을 극대화하는 운동에 가깝고. 이해했어?"

That single line kills the ladder anyone reaches for first
(편측 > 프리웨이트+복합 > 머신+복합 > 머신+고립). Pilates carries almost no
load and maximal coordination, so a ladder built on load ranks it nowhere and
scores it ZERO. Free weights CORRELATE with coordination demand; the
correlation is not the definition, and using it as one makes every unloaded
controlled movement disappear.

    wrong   coordination = how free the weight is   (derived from load)
    right   coordination = an independent axis; load may be zero

So the axis is read as SEPARATE sub-measures, each in its own unit, and they are
never blended into a single score (blending is the flattening recorded in
design notes 2026-08-24 (goal consult conversation) section 9 -- the
picture becomes a number and the number then gets mistaken for the picture):

  1. 편측 비중          one side at a time, AT ANY LOAD.
                        Classifier: exercise-enter.extract_record_params.
  2. 무하중 버티기 비중 timed holds carrying NO external load.
                        Classifier: rx-calc._is_isometric_hold for work slots;
                        `hold_sec` with no `drills.<code>.implement` for
                        준비운동/쿨다운 drills.
  3. 느린 제어 비율     work sets done at a slow controlled tempo, loaded or
                        not (2026-09-30). Classifier: is_controlled_tempo over
                        the slot `tempo`, else ax_exercise.tempo.

PILATES IS SUB-MEASURE 3, NOT 2 (2026-09-30). The first cut let the hold
counter stand for Pilates; Pilates is slow, controlled, full-range movement,
not a hold (kimwog exercise-ideal-pilates-controlled-movement-037). The
connection strand's measure set is 1 + 3 (편측·느린 제어 세트 비율); 2 is still
read and printed, as holds.

Sub-measure 2 is counted across ALL THREE blocks, 준비운동 and 쿨다운 included.
Those blocks are (correctly) excluded from VOLUME, and because the census read
day_work_items() -- the volume-eligible collection -- their coordination
contribution read 0. The owner's own program is the proof: wall_sit is
2 weekly sets of exactly this, and the 내전근 아이소메트릭 drill runs on three
of the four days. All of it read as zero coordination.

    "볼륨은 크기를 위한거야. 다른 각자의 기여도는 각자의 값들이 있는거지."

VOLUME IS UNCHANGED BY ANY OF THIS. day_work_items() remains the single
volume-eligible collection and nothing here feeds it; a coordination set is not
a volume set. Counting them together would re-commit the error that scored
functional work against a hypertrophy band.

WHAT IS NOT MEASURED IS NAMED, NOT ZEROED. An unloaded drill with no control
marker (a dynamic mat drill, say) plausibly carries coordination and has no
honest number here. It is listed under `unmeasured` rather than silently
counted as 0 -- the same discipline migration 033 applied to a zero-metric
axis. An unmeasured thing that vanishes from the readout is how this gap
survived for months.

stdlib only. Execute-only: nothing here is imported into a model context.
"""

